                <h2>1. Build a Master Ledger for Every Ad Account</h2>
<p>Before reconciliation begins, operations should record every advertising platform, account, payment method, and invoicing entity in a shared ledger. Required fields include the platform, account ID, company or business unit, currency, time zone, payer, recharge method, billing cycle, legal invoice name, tax ID, operations owner, and finance contact.</p>
<p>If one ad account is funded through multiple regions, currencies, or payment cards, each funding route should be recorded separately. Balances held by Meta, Google, TikTok, and other platforms are generally tracked by account and should not be treated as one combined cash pool without verification.</p>
<p>The ledger should also include three controls: whether automatic recharges are enabled, the balance alert threshold, and the escalation contact. Before enabling automatic recharges, confirm the card balance, recharge limit, and billing frequency to prevent both campaign disruption and repeated charges.</p>
<h2>2. Reconcile Four Sets of Records</h2>
<p>Effective reconciliation requires simultaneous review of cash records, platform balances, advertising spend, and invoices.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cash records:</strong> Actual charges, payment fees, credited amount, recharge date, and payment order number.</li>
<li><strong>Platform records:</strong> Amount credited to each account, promotional or credit balances, current balance, and platform adjustments.</li>
<li><strong>Spend records:</strong> Advertising charges, invalid-traffic refunds, reversals, promotional credit usage, and balance changes.</li>
<li><strong>Invoice records:</strong> Invoice amount, tax, service period, invoice number, status, and related payment amount.</li>
</ul>
<p>Assign a unique reference to every recharge and match it using the ad account ID and recharge order number. Use the following relationship as a control: opening balance + net funds received + refunds or credits - ad spend ± platform adjustments = closing balance. If the result differs, check currency conversion, delayed posting, tax deductions, and rounding before using the next period's balance to hide the difference.</p>
<h2>3. Run the Daily Spend Reconciliation</h2>
<p>After campaigns run each day, operations should export the platform spend report while finance updates the cash ledger. Match both records by date, account, currency, and campaign.</p>
<h3>Daily Checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li>Confirm that yesterday's recharges were credited, and record delayed postings and failed orders.</li>
<li>Keep platform-reported spend separate from internal billing and attribution data; do not use platform figures to infer actual payments.</li>
<li>Check whether any account is below its balance threshold. Before replenishing funds, verify that the selected payment method is active and available.</li>
<li>Flag negative balances, unusual refunds, expiring promotional credit, and sudden increases in spend.</li>
<li>Save supporting records and document the approval result for unplanned or high-value charges.</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, one cross-border e-commerce team initially identified a significant difference between two days of platform spend. The cause was not a duplicate charge: two campaign batches were settled in different currencies. After adding original currency, settlement currency, and conversion date to the ledger, the team prevented an exchange-rate variance from being misclassified as an operational anomaly.</p>
<h2>4. Match Statements and Invoices at Month-End</h2>
<p>After the billing date, a platform may first issue a spending statement and later issue the formal invoice. Tax adjustments, refunds, or supplemental charges may also appear between the two documents. Finance should review each account separately rather than checking only the platform's total.</p>
<h3>Four Month-End Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li>Lock the confirmed recharges, spend, refunds, and platform adjustments for the period.</li>
<li>Cross-check the statement total against the sum of line items and confirm that the account count matches.</li>
<li>Verify the invoice name, tax ID, currency, service period, tax amount, and invoice number.</li>
<li>Match the invoice to the payment record and mark it as paid, partially paid, unpaid, or pending verification.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the invoice amount differs from the payment amount, classify the difference before taking action. Common categories include payment fees, prepaid tax, platform balance credits, promotional credit, timing differences, exchange-rate movements, and invoice adjustments. If the invoice name or tax ID is incorrect, request a correction or replacement through the platform before initiating payment.</p>
<h2>5. Establish Exception Handling and Risk Controls</h2>
<p>Record every discrepancy in an issue log, including the detection date, affected account, amount, owner, and status. Set response times by cause: allow system delays to clear through the next settlement cycle; contact the payment provider immediately for duplicate charges; pause affected campaigns for abnormal spend; and coordinate with finance and platform support for invoice errors.</p>
<p>Before releasing payment, complete three checks: verify that the account belongs to the correct invoicing entity, confirm that the invoice amount matches the service period, and check for refunded, frozen, or reversed recharge orders. High-risk accounts should have unlimited automatic recharging disabled, with balance alerts, payment notifications, and operation logs enabled.</p>
<p>Operations should confirm the campaign and balance data, while finance should confirm payments, taxes, and invoices. Close the reconciliation period only after both parties review and approve the records. The objective is not to make every amount appear identical, but to ensure that every recharge has a destination, every charge has supporting evidence, and every invoice is traceable.</p>
